hed helper makes DEDENT zero-width with `positionedToken(DEDENT, getCharIndex(), getCharIndex() - 1)` (`manifest.edn` `:patches`, `Python3LexerBase.java:62`), so the exporter emits `{:type \"DEDENT\" :text \"\" :span [16 16] :synthetic true}` for the required indentation fixture: the source slice at `[16 16]` is `\"\"`, equal to the text, and the preceding NEWLINE ends at 16, so neither justification holds and the fixture of §5.1 fails M1. The converse is also unsound: collapsing any misplaced real token to a zero-width span makes its text differ from the empty slice, so v2's rule cannot catch a falsely synthetic NAME. Root cause: synthetic-ness is a **grammar role** (the helper constructs these tokens; their text is not source evidence), and v2 tried to re-derive it from text. Further evidence that text is not evidence: the reviewer's probe on `\"if x:\\n    y = 1\"` yields NEWLINE `\"<EOF>\"`, DEDENT `\"<EOF>\"` and EOF `\"y = 1\"` as `:text`, all runtime `getText` fallbacks of zero-width or back-computed tokens. | §1.3.1, §1.3.6, §2.1, §5.1, §5.2 |\n+| 11 | P1 | **Conceded.** v2 §1.3 (error outcomes) required `:line`/`:column` to identify the position at span start. `cst.clj:137-154` records the listener's `line`/`column` arguments verbatim and computes `:span` from the offending token's char indexes. For helper-constructed tokens the two name different places by construction: `positionedToken` builds a `CommonToken` from the lexer's source pair, whose constructor copies the lexer's **current** line and column (the start of the next line, since the NEWLINE lexeme has been consumed), and `createDedent` overrides DEDENT's line with the last real token's line (`Python3LexerBase.java:62-64`). Reproduced: `\"if x:\\n    y =\\nz = 2\\n\"` → `{:line 3 :column 0 :span [13 14]}`, byte 13 being line 2 column 7; and on a DEDENT, `\"if x:\\n    return\\n    else\\nz = 2\\n\"` → `{:message \"mismatched input '' expecting INDENT\" :line 3 :column 0 :span [26 26]}`, byte 26 being line 4 column 0. The JS and Dart `CommonToken` constructors copy line/column from the source pair the same way, so this is the pinned helper's behaviour on every target, not a JVM defect to fix before goldens. `yang.antlr.md` §5.2 already rules it: \"Canonical spans use half-open offsets into the original source bytes... Line and column values are presentation projections.\" | §1.3.7, §1.4, §5.1, §5.3 |\n \n@@ -73,15 +39,27 @@\n \n-## Delta vs v1 (what the reviewer should re-read)\n-\n-- New: §0 ledger; §1.2 outcome-specific `validate!`; §1.3 `conform` against source and\n-  vocabulary; §2.3 isolation mechanism per runtime, fallback removed; §2.4 portable probe;\n-  §3.4 build ordering and both classpaths; §3.5 pin enforcement and lockfiles; §4.2 missing\n-  target mechanism with a Java-only demonstration; §5 rewritten (genuine goldens, diagnostic\n-  parity rules); §6.2 browser decision; §7 eligibility condition; §10 consumer inventory and\n-  demo migration; §11 resequenced (M0 executable acceptance, `parser.cljc` moves to M1,\n-  browser and demo work in M2).\n-- Unchanged: the ruling that the IR is CST packet v1 (§1.1), the exporter contract (§2.1),\n-  the manifest shape apart from `:runtime` (§3.1), the boundary (§8), the owner\n-  requirements, the lanes policy.\n-- Corrected statements: §9 (TS/PHP helper facts, vendoring exception, licences).\n+## Delta vs v2 (what the reviewer should re-read)\n+\n+- New: §1.3.1 synthetic policy as grammar-role data on the profile; §1.3.6 rewritten\n+  (role-keyed justification, canonical synthetic text, the mutation table); §1.3.7 the\n+  error-record contract: two coordinate systems, one invariant each, no cross-identity;\n+  §2.1 canonical synthetic text in `token-record` (the one JVM exporter edit) and\n+  `:synthetic-policy` in the profile contract; §5.1 corpus gains the helper-NEWLINE,\n+  helper-DEDENT and EOF-offending error sources; §5.2 layer 1 gains the acceptance and\n+  mutation tests of §1.3.6 and the error-